3.5  SELinux and AppArmor

TL;DR
  - Both add a second set of rules on top of rwx: even a process running as root
    can only do what its policy allows.
  - SELinux (Fedora, RHEL) labels every file and process. AppArmor (Ubuntu,
    Debian) gives profiles to programs, by path.
  - A denial looks like a "Permission denied" that rwx can't explain: look in
    the audit log.

      getenforce                       # SELinux: Enforcing or Permissive
      sudo aa-status                   # AppArmor: profiles and their mode
      sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent  # recent SELinux denials

On your machine
  Modules   capability, landlock, yama, apparmor
  AppArmor  enabled · profiles: 2 enforce, 1 complain

Pitfalls
  - Don't turn SELinux off to fix a problem. Switch to permissive for a test
    (sudo setenforce 0), find the denial, then fix the label or the policy.
  - A file moved with mv keeps its old SELinux label; a copy gets the folder's.
    sudo restorecon -Rv /path puts the expected labels back.
  - Serving web files from an unusual folder needs the right SELinux label or
    boolean (setsebool), on top of the usual rights.
  - sudo aa-complain PROFILE makes one AppArmor profile log instead of block, to
    debug a single program.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 3.5 --why

Related
  Commands  getenforce, setenforce, aa-status, restorecon
  Paths     /etc/selinux/config, /etc/apparmor.d/
  Hub       idk permissions Permissions
  See also  idk 3.1 rwx, octal and umask
  See also  idk 3.4 ACLs and capabilities
